WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
[TRAFFIC NOISE]

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:08.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.580
Go across the floor.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.510
Look away.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.410
That's better.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.700
No shoulders, no shoulders.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.380
Good.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.960
So it's soft.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.660
1, 2, 3, 1, 2-- like you're hugging

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.875
the ground with your legs.

00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:26.670
Virginia Johnson, the first African-American prima

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.880
ballerina, and was basically told, you're great,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.470
but you're not white.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.810
When I was growing up, we would, like,

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.320
sit in a circle and watch, riveted, Virginia Johnson

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.220
dancing.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.015
It was such a huge part of my ballet education.

00:00:42.015 --> 00:00:44.190
So Chelsea, what was your favorite thing to do?

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.827
Was it jumping, turning, Adagio?

00:00:46.827 --> 00:00:47.335
Ooh.

00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:48.127
Oh, not the Adagio.

00:00:48.127 --> 00:00:49.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.680
I've always had too much energy.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.180
The idea that you might make it in traditional ballet company

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.890
was kind of farfetched.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.930
It took a lot of inner strength.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.640
It took a lot of what we call gutsiness.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.830
I mean, you've spent more than 50 years

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.550
creating it and proving it and opening doors,

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.260
sometimes having to knock him down.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.450
The first time I saw a Black dancer on stage,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.440
and I wanted to be that.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.330
And seeing someone that looks like you,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.630
it just gives you that, like, go-ahead

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.490
to be able to achieve your dreams.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.380
How old were you when you saw a Black dancer?

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:22.980
I was five.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.080
I told my mom, I was like, Mom, I want to do that.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.080
And she was like, well, there's the Dance Theater of Harlem.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.890
But in 1969, Arthur Mitchell created the Dance Theater

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.670
of Harlem, DTH, right here in New York City, which

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.810
at the time was the only venue centered on Black and Brown

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.280
dancers.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.490
I've read enough about what Arthur Mitchell and you

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.940
and the others who were in on the creation were up against.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.430
And here we are, in this rehearsal space,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.630
and it used to be a garage.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.380
(LAUGHING) Yes.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.260
Some of these students that we're teaching

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.860
have never been in a studio.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.420
Oh my.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:00.780
Oh, wow.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.300
So what is that going to be when they actually come into a room,

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.590
and they can move across the floor?

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:05.640
It's going to be amazing.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.965
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:11.900
OK, here we go.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:19.030
We were in Brazil last year, and we

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.715
went to Poland and Lithuania and all these great places,

00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:23.980
you know, being able to see different audiences

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.220
and touch different lives.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.850
I did not speak English at all.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.070
Like, I was like--

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:30.880
signs.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.305
But she could dance.

00:02:32.305 --> 00:02:33.310
She could dance.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.620
That's the universal language, right?

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:41.050
We're passing it on to show that it is reality for any color,

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.475
any race, any background.

00:02:43.475 --> 00:02:44.600
Oh, she jumped.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.995
I wanted to soar, but I was trying very hard to not move.

00:02:47.995 --> 00:02:49.370
It's the closest thing to flying.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:50.840
It felt like freedom.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.340
Right?

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.360
This is Harlem now.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.790
We are Harlem now.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:55.595
OK, one more time.

00:02:55.595 --> 00:02:56.095
Same thing.

00:03:00.637 --> 00:03:02.220
You know, what we've been watching you

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.070
practice is from a ballet called Passage.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.590
DTH commissioned the ballet, Passage,

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:12.970
to commemorate the 400th anniversary of 1619,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:16.930
working with two women of color, choreographer Claudia Schreier,

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.780
and composer Jessie Montgomery.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.760
It is depicting people being abducted and thrown

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.620
into slaveships.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.470
The ballet is really thinking about what

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.950
it was to be on that first ship landing here.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:38.230
The music is just so exquisite.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.940
It's an augmented string quartet.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.327
So it's a very small orchestration.

00:03:42.327 --> 00:03:42.910
It's intimate.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:43.480
It's intimate.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:43.980
Yes.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.570
Painful, and also uplifting.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.490
Embodying the Middle Passage and what

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:51.330
that meant for our ancestors, you move forward,

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.080
and you're reaching up, and there's, like,

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.530
a desperation and a hope, and it just really encapsulates

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.570
all this human emotions.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.280
Especially at a time when there are

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:04.330
lots of states that don't want us to actually teach children

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.700
historically accurate American history.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.560
I grew up in a state like that, so

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.220
coming to DTH has been really interesting and wonderful

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:13.750
and deepening for that reason.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.160
It needed to be part of everybody's awareness.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:16.660
Right.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.702
Because it changes you when you know the history.

00:04:18.702 --> 00:04:19.690
Right.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.140
The place you first performed it was Norfolk, Virginia.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:27.310
That was one of the places where the first slaves were

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:27.970
delivered.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:28.930
Hampton Roads.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.750
And the people of Virginia wanted

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.480
to recognize what had happened.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.040
Sitting in the house that night, I

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:40.540
could feel the excitement turn to, oh, wait,

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:43.570
there's something else happening here,

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:48.610
where you felt everybody just go, oh my god, I actually

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.110
get emotional when I think about it.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.195
We all know about the story of slavery,

00:04:52.195 --> 00:04:54.070
and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:58.070
But it's that remote kind of-- it's a history thing,

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.905
but through this work, it became real.

00:05:09.845 --> 00:05:11.220
The beautiful thing about Passage

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.480
is, it's not a ballet that goes, ugh, bad things happened.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.560
It's a very uplifting ballet.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.555
You go on a journey and passage.

00:05:26.055 --> 00:05:29.030
It gives you an opportunity to experience

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:33.800
a sense of loss, of uncertainty, but also hope and possibility.

00:05:44.955 --> 00:05:47.030
So you guys like to dance?

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:47.700
Yeah.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:48.200
Yes?

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.867
Are you guys going to come dance with us

00:05:49.867 --> 00:05:51.370
at the Dance Theater of Harlem?

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.182
You guys are very good.

00:05:53.182 --> 00:05:53.682
Thank you.

00:05:53.682 --> 00:05:54.182
Thank you.

00:05:54.182 --> 00:05:55.610
We want to see your moves now.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:56.485
Show me what you got.

00:06:00.273 --> 00:06:00.990
Yes!

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:02.520
Oh, that was a nice sosay.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.190
Very good.

